standing Retention Policies in Northgate Vault\n\nA retention policy determines how long Northgate Vault keeps a document before it becomes\neligible for permanent deletion. Every document inherits exactly one retention policy, and that\npolicy is resolved at the moment the document is created. Changing a policy later affects only\ndocuments created after the change; it never retroactively alters the disposition date of a\ndocument that already exists. This behaviour is deliberate, because retroactive changes would\nmake it impossible to prove to an auditor what the disposition schedule was at any given point\nin the past.\n\nPolicies are evaluated in a fixed order. The system first looks for a policy attached directly to\nthe document. If none exists, it looks for a policy attached to the folder that contains the\ndocument, then to that folder's parent, and so on up to the workspace root. If no policy is found\nanywhere on that path, the workspace default applies. The workspace default cannot be removed,\nonly changed, which guarantees that every document always resolves to some policy.\n\nThere are three kinds of disposition. Soft deletion moves the document to a recycle bin where it\nremains visible to administrators for a further thirty days. Hard deletion removes the document\ncontent but keeps an immutable audit stub recording that a document existed, who created it, and\nwhen it was disposed. Purge removes everything, including the audit stub, and is only available\nto workspaces operating under a legal regime that requires it; most customers should never enable\npurge, because it destroys the evidence that the retention policy was followed.\n\nA legal hold suspends all disposition for the documents it covers. Holds take precedence over\nevery policy, including purge, and they cannot be overridden by an administrator. Only a user with\nthe Legal Hold Officer role may place or release a hold, and every placement and release is\nwritten to the audit log with the reason supplied by that user. When a hold is released, the\naffected documents return to their normal schedule; if their disposition date has already passed,\nthey become eligible immediately and will be processed in the next nightly sweep.\n\nThe nightly sweep runs at 02:00 in the workspace's configured timezone. It processes documents in\ncreation order, oldest first, and stops after four hours regardless of how much work remains.\nAnything not processed is picked up by the next sweep. Because the sweep is time-bounded rather\nthan work-bounded, a workspace with a very large backlog may take several nights to catch up, and\nadministrators sometimes mistake this for a failure. The sweep report, available under Reports and\nAudit, shows exactly how many documents were considered, how many were disposed, and how many\nremained in the queue when the sweep stopped.\n\nTwo questions come up repeatedly in support tickets. The first is why a document with an expired\ndisposition date is still visible. Almost always the answer is either an active legal hold or a\nbacklogged sweep, both of which the sweep report will show. The second is whether a retention\npolicy can be shortened. It can, but only for future documents, and the interface deliberately\nmakes this awkward in order to prevent an administrator from accidentally believing that existing\ndocuments have been reshuffled onto the shorter schedule.\n", "NAME                                 READY   STATUS      RESTARTS   AGE     IP              NODE\napi-server-7d9f8b00-a0000            1/1     Running     0          2d0h    10.244.0.0      node-0.cluster.internal\napi-server-7d9f8b01-b0003            1/1     Running     1          3d1h    10.244.1.11     node-1.cluster.internal\napi-server-7d9f8b02-c0006            1/1     Pending     2          4d2h    10.244.2.22     node-2.cluster.internal\napi-server-7d9f8b03-d0009            1/1     Running     3          5d3h    10.244.3.33     node-3.cluster.internal\napi-se